  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThe next shortwave will cause a weak cold front to dip down into \ncentral IL on Thursday, then retreat back north as a warm front \nFriday. There could be some sprinkles or light rain across the \nnorthern part of the forecast area Thursday afternoon. Precip \nchances are greatest Friday with the warm front, near and north of I-\n72 (40-70% chance). More rain chances remain in the forecast through \nthe weekend. Whatever light rain manages to fall between now and \nSunday will do little to alleviate the ongoing dry stretch.\n\nThe start of November will usher in a significant pattern change. A \nmuch stronger low pressure system is shown to move through early \nnext week. This system will bring the greatest chances for showers \nand thunderstorms to the area within the next week. The NBM has a \nwidespread 50-70% chance across central and southeastern IL focused \non Monday evening into Tuesday evening. Anomalous PWATs for November \nare forecast for this system's passage, values 1.5-1.8 inches on \nvarious models. The LREF shows a 50-60% chance areawide for over 1 \ninch of rain to fall in the next 7 days.\n\nTemperatures will continue to be above normal for the next few days, \nthen will trend slightly cooler starting Saturday into next week. \n\"Cooler\" in this sense means temperatures will be in the upper 60s \nto low 70s for highs, which is still above normal. The CPC's 6-10 \nday, 8-14 day, and Week 3-4 outlooks are all continuing to show \nabove normal temperatures. The additional cloud cover and increased \nrain chances this weekend into next week may inhibit the \ntemperatures from getting too warm, potentially being cooler than \nwhat is in the forecast now.",
